The primary authorization flaw is in the `update_subscription` endpoint (lines 81-106), which performs Stripe billing mutations (cancel/resubscribe) on a customer identified by a user-controlled `email` parameter. While it requires `@requires(["authenticated"])`, there's no ownership check — any authenticated user can cancel or resubscribe another user's Stripe subscription by supplying their email. This is a classic CWE-639 (IDOR) — the object (Stripe customer) is fetched by a user-controlled identifier without verifying the requester owns it.

There is no explicit policy rule forbidding this, but the authorization model implicitly requires that a user can only modify their own subscription. The fact that the endpoint takes an arbitrary `email` parameter (rather than using the authenticated user's identity) is the flaw.

